  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 132 AM CDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nLittle change in the overall pattern expected through much of the \nforecast period as South Texas remains under the influence of mid-\nlevel ridging. Sufficient moisture will remain in place for isolated \nshowers and thunderstorms to develop along the sea breeze each \nafternoon, primarily across the Coastal Plains. Activity may \noccasionally push farther inland, through most locations will remain \ndry. Rain chances will generally remain low (20-25%) through the \nweekend and into next week. \n\nAbove-normal temperatures will continue through the period, with hot \nafternoons and warm overnight lows expected. Heat index values will \ngenerally remain below 110 degrees, though locally higher values \ncannot be ruled out. Continue to practice heat safety when spending \ntime outdoors. \n\nForecast uncertainty begins to increase toward the middle of next \nweek as guidance hints at a possible disturbance approaching the \nregion. Confidence remains too low at this time regarding its \nevolution and potential impacts to South Texas.",
